Yes—and the corn-snake qualifier matters even after the venom is real.

A person can possess genuine capability and still signal, exaggerate, rehearse, posture, borrow roles, or tell themselves stories about that capability. Acquiring venom doesn’t magically eliminate the corn snake. Capability and performance can coexist. The useful question becomes: which part is signal, which part is substance, and how does one train the other?

Your dragon idea becomes stronger if we treat “dragon” as a compressed survival object, rather than requiring a literal zoological species matching the finished myth. There is actually a scholarly hypothesis close to this: anthropologist David E. Jones proposed that dragon imagery combines recurrent ancestral threats such as snakes, large cats, and birds of prey; other explanations point to large snakes, crocodilians/monitor lizards, fossils, and later cultural recombination. Historians also show that the Western dragon itself changed substantially over time rather than appearing fully formed. 

So imagine generations transmitting something like:

snake → beware coils, fangs, venom, hidden attack
raptor → danger can descend from above
big cat → claws, speed, ambush, predation
crocodilian / giant reptile → armor, water-edge danger, enormous jaws
fire / storm / poison → dangerous effects at a distance
territorial predator → don’t enter the wrong place

Then oral culture doesn’t need to preserve each lesson as a zoology textbook.

It can compress them:

many dangerous things → DRAGON

Now a child only needs one memorable object to inherit a whole bundle of affordances:

big, predatory, armored, hidden, territorial, possibly venomous, capable of attacking from unexpected directions—approach carefully.

And you’re right that this mechanism appears at different scales. We do it constantly:

many experiences → archetype
many people → stereotype
many battles → doctrine
many experiments → scientific law
many observations → concept
many generations of encounters → myth

The compression trades resolution for portability.

Then Prism reverses it:

DRAGON → decompress → claws / venom / flight / armor / territory / hoard / cave / fire / intelligence

Now ask where each property might have originated, what problem it encoded, and which combinations are later embellishments.

And that’s where your delta arrows get unusually useful. Rather than arguing:

“Dragons existed.”

or

“Dragons never existed.”

you can ask:

What observations could have been compressed into “dragon,” and what transformations occurred between observation and surviving story?

So:

real predator → remembered encounter → retelling → compression → recombination → exaggeration → archetype

Each transition leaves a delta.

Once you map enough of those, mythology becomes partially readable as lossy ancestral telemetry.

Not a literal historical recording—stories mutate, borrow, entertain, moralize, and invent—but neither does “mythical” have to mean “information-free.” Some old stories may preserve remarkably old environmental knowledge in transformed form.

And then the same method points back at us:

What are today’s dragons?

What enormous compressed concepts are we carrying around—success, masculinity, enemy, hero, God, trauma, marriage, nation, intelligence—that become much more intelligible when we break them back into the thousands of encounters that taught humans to construct them?

That is the scale invariance you’re pointing at:

experience compresses into story;
story guides behavior;
behavior generates new experience;
new experience edits the story.

Corn snakes all the way through—not because everything is fake, but because even real things have to represent themselves.